    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d718">

                <pb facs="0992" n="978" xml:id="pg978"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.24/7–1744: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Ambassador in China</hi>
                        (<hi rendition="#italic">Gauss</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1944-07-17T14:00:00-04:00">July 17, 1944—2
                            p.m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>965. Referring to Department’s telegram<anchor n="26" xml:id="d718-f1"/> in
                    regard to new Chinese Supply Commission, Department would appreciate receiving,
                    for its own information and possible use in discussions with American
                    businessmen, Embassy’s views on following: (1) does Commission speak for
                    National Government on questions relating to post-war reconstruction projects
                    negotiated in this country or does it represent merely a group or agency in the
                    Government; (2) if the former, do all projects negotiated in this country need
                    to have Commission’s approval?</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Hull</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
